README-KEEP -- taking programs home==================================    keep <name>...      the program, and every file it needs    keep -n <name>...   say what would be written; write nothing    keep -f <name>...   overwrite files already there    keep -s <name>...   take its source tree too    keep -p <dir> ...   write under <dir> instead of /h1    kept                every file keep has written    unkeep <name>...    remove what keep wrote for it    unkeep -n <name>... say what would go    unkeep -a <name>... remove your saves and scores for it tooEverything runs in place from /dd; keep is for taking a program to yourown disk, mounted as /h1 (DOC/README-RUNNING).  It copies the program andthe files DOC/DEPENDS says it reads, laid out as the program expects: aprogram that reads /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz gets /h1/GAMES/adv/glorkz, so itfinds its file when your disk is /dd again.WHERE FROM, WHERE TOFrom the collection -- the first of /dd, /h0, /h1, /h2 holdingDOC/DEPENDS -- to /h1.  `-p <dir>', or KEEP_FROM and KEEP_TO in theenvironment, say otherwise.  Set those inside the OS-9 session: os9execdoes not pass on the variables of the shell that started it.WHAT IT TAKES    keep cookie     cookie, its sayings and its sayhash    keep fortune    fortune and its fortunes.dat    keep hack       hack and its whole playground, with room to saveA data directory is copied whole.  A top-level system directory -- CMDS,SYS, LIB, USR, LOG -- is not: your disk has its own, and keep names it andleaves it.  Termcap comes along only when your disk has none.`keep -s' takes the source where it survived.  Source trees are named byarchive, not program (SRC/divutils holds gen, run and if); keep looks thename up in DOC/ORIGINS, and `man -w <name>' shows it first.Nothing of yours is overwritten.  A file of your own under the same namestops keep, which names it and moves on; `-f' overrides.  Keeping a programtwice restores what is missing and touches nothing you have changed.NAMES YOUR OS-9 HAS TOOMeasured against a Microware CMDS, these names exist on both:    chown    cio      compress  csl      dir      dump     expand   grep    help     kermit   make      math     math881  mshell   mv       printenv    setime   su       tar      umacs     undel    whatcio, csl, math and math881 are Microware's own modules, the same as yours.The rest are different programs that share a name: this `dir' is not your`dir'.  keep will not replace yours unless you say `-f', and then yours isgone -- copy it aside first.Data files are written publicly writable, so a game can save for any user,not only the super-user.UNKEEPEvery file keep writes is recorded in SYS/kept on the destination: forwhich program, where, its size and its checksum.  unkeep reads it, and foreach file:    gone already              says so    another program needs it  leaves it, drops this program's claim    unchanged                 removes it    changed since             keeps it, and says soSo a termcap two programs share stays until the last one goes, and yoursaves and scores survive.  `unkeep -a' removes changed files too.    $ keep fortune    collection at /dd, keeping into /h1    fortune:      /h1/CMDS/fortune      /h1/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat    2 files written.    $ echo "A fortune I added myself." >> /h1/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat    $ unkeep fortune    fortune:      removed /h1/CMDS/fortune      !! /h1/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat has CHANGED since it was kept -- left alone         (your saves and high scores survive unkeep; -a removes them too)    1 removed, 1 left alone.A file left alone stays on the record for a later unkeep.  The checksum isthe OS-9 module CRC, used on ordinary files too.keep, kept and unkeep are one C source, SRC/keep/keep.c, built trap-free:they need no cio.  The bash scripts they replaced are kept beside it.